The board state has no immediate winning move for either player, and the only cell that completes four-in-a-row for x is the presently inaccessible square (2,4). Consequently the sole dangerous column for the present player is column 4: occupying (3,4) would make (2,4) droppable, after which x wins at once. Column 4 is therefore illegal for o.

The only moves that create an immediate threat (an accessible cell that would complete four-in-a-row for o on the following turn) are the two ends of the open diagonal whose present occupants are (2,2)=o and (3,1)=o:

- o in column 0 (landing on (4,0)) produces the three-in-a-row (4,0)–(3,1)–(2,2) whose open end (1,3) is the next free cell of column 3;
- o in column 3 (landing on (1,3)) produces the symmetric three-in-a-row whose open end is (4,0).

Both threats are single, so x can answer them. The continuation that begins with column 0, however, yields a second forced reply:

1. o plays 0 (threatens 3);
2. x must play 3;
3. o plays 0 again, now building three vertical stones in column 0 (threatens the next cell of the same column);
4. x must play 0.

After these four forced half-moves the position still contains no immediate win for x, o has gained a tempo relative to any line that begins with the opposite end of the diagonal, and o’s stones on the left side of the board are far more advanced. All other legal columns create no threat at all and therefore surrender the initiative to x’s only forcing idea (column 4). Hence column 0 is the unique move that retains pressure and maximises o’s long-term prospects.

